WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.400
Why shut down a well-liked series?

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.420
Because actors leave, creators finish telling
their stories, or a network is ready to move

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:06.920
on.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.300
Here's a look at all the TV shows that will
have told the stories they needed to tell

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.660
by the end of 2019.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.580
But be warned: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.640
Legion simply isn't the kind of show that
can go on and on forever.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.140
For one thing, the possibly real, possibly
not-real adventures of Dan Stevens' character

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.560
David Haller are just so wildly out there,
there's no way creator Noah Hawley and his

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.100
writing staff could've kept up the pace indefinitely.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.480
After all, the story involves a man who spends
much of his time in a mental institution and

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.200
doesn't quite have a firm grip on reality…or
his burgeoning superpowers.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.579
"Who were the people at the hospital that
day?"

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.920
"No, no, no, no, you're, you're lying, trying..."

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.700
"David…"

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.940
It's up to the writers to advance the story
to a certain place, especially since Legion

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.120
relates to the world of the X-Men: David is
evidently the mutant son of the famed Professor

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:51.720
X.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.460
FX announced that the twisty, turny, highly-engrossing
sci-fi hit will conclude with its upcoming

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.200
third season, which began airing on June 24th.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.500
Fortunately, it sounds like this is all in
accord with Hawley's master plan: He evidently

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.900
wanted the series to only run for three seasons.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.220
Speaking at a Television Critics Association
event in February 2019, he said:

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.900
There's no denying that Batman has always
been a popular character.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.940
The never-not brooding Bruce Wayne and his
rogue's gallery of colorful villains are bonafide

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.600
pop culture fixtures, no matter how you slice
it.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.409
There are decades' worth of comic books.

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:30.710
The Tim Burton films from the '80s and '90s.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.180
A slew of animated shows.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.660
And, of course, Christopher Nolan's truly
iconic Dark Knight trilogy.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.900
And then there's Gotham, a clever reimagining
of the Batman story that chronicled the adventures

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.810
of a young Bruce Wayne, and also introduced
us to younger versions of Commissioner Gordon,

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.240
the Joker, the Penguin, and the Riddler.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.630
Since the show was always building towards
young Bruce Wayne's ultimate transformation

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.680
into Batman, it was a concept that eventually
had to reach its foregone conclusion.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.720
Evidently recognizing that fact, Fox renewed
Gotham for a fifth and decidedly final season

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:02.320
during the presentation of its 2018-2019 programming
slate.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.890
Honestly, Gotham fans should feel lucky the
show got another season at all.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:12.060
It ranked a not-so great Number 129 in the
2017-2018 TV ratings.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.380
It also managed to survive a cancellation
massacre at Fox that involved execs axing

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.940
Brooklyn Nine-Nine, The Mick, Lucifer, and
The Exorcist, all in one fell swoop.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.120
Fortunately, Brooklyn Nine-Nine and Lucifer
were ultimately rescued by NBC and Netflix,

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.420
respectively…and Gotham got a chance to
wrap up its story on Fox.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.040
On the surface, Broad City was a wild and
wacky comedy about two young women dealing

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.690
with nutty characters and situations in New
York City, but underneath all the zaniness,

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.100
its five-season run was low-key groundbreaking.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.100
It's arguably the first New York-based comedy
that ultimately didn't glamorize New York.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.020
In every episode, co-creators Abbi Jacobson
and Ilana Glazer portrayed two kooky young

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.900
women battling a grimy, confusing, apathetic,
and ludicrously expensive Big Apple.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.700
But it's funny!

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.500
"Hello sir, we'd like to report that the sound
was really subpar."

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.200
"Woof!"

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.300
"The Dolby was nowhere near 5.1…Great, okay."

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.080
An endlessly entertaining and eccentric comedy
series, Broad City was, at its core, all about

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.880
the deep unconditional love shared between
these two best friends.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.640
Broad City perfectly encapsulated how friendship
often propels you through your twenties…particularly

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.840
when you're flailing in the big city, and
on your own for the very first time.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.011
Ultimately, the co-stars and co-creators aged
out of that period in their lives, and so

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.160
did their characters.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.000
That's why the show had to come to an end.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.680
As Jacobson told TV Line:

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.260
The Big Bang Theory's gang of socially awkward
California scientists always managed to bring

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.200
in huge numbers, even at the end of its 11th
season.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.380
In fact, it was the top-rated show on broadcast
TV for the 2017-2018 season.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.300
In advance of the show's 12th season, the
season in which the show surpassed Cheers

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:57.980
and became the longest-running multi-camera
sitcom in history, TV execs began negotiating

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.880
seasons 13 and 14.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.260
And that's when actor Jim Parsons balked,
despite the fact that he was being paid roughly

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.290
$1 million per episode to portray quirky genius
Sheldon Cooper.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.040
A well-deserved salary, to be sure.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.640
According to Entertainment Weekly, the four-time
Emmy winner effectively said no to $50 million

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.940
dollars when he turned down two more seasons.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.539
According to Deadline, Big Bang co-creator
and executive producer Chuck Lorre gathered

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.780
the cast in his office after a table read
in August 2018.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.880
That's when a tearful Parsons told his castmates
that he intended to leave the show at the

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:29.900
end of the season.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:33.940
And then Lorre announced that the 12th season
would, in fact, be the last one for everybody.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.540
He preferred ending The Big Bang Theory altogether
rather than continue on without Jim Parsons.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.220
Or Sheldon Cooper, for that matter.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.900
HBO's monster hit Game of Thrones was quite
possibly the last true "appointment viewing"

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.180
to ever air on television.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.440
In the age of Netflix binge-watching and DVRs,
millions gathered to watch the show when it

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:55.300
first aired on HBO Go on Sunday nights...both
to avoid online spoilers, and to immediately

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.569
find out what was happening with Jon Snow,
the Lannisters, and Daenerys Targaryen, the

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:00.580
Mother of Dragons.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.840
Based on the series of bestselling fantasy
novels by George R.R. Martin, the Westeros

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.320
saga reeled viewers in from its very first
episode in 2011.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.200
But the eighth season, which consisted of
six oversized episodes, was definitely the

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.160
show's last.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.420
At least audiences finally know who was destined
to sit on the Iron Throne: No one!

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.640
But why would HBO force millions of people
to live in a cold, empty world without the

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.600
promise of a new Game of Thrones episode?

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.180
Well, the show reportedly concluded because
season eight was the end of the calculated

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:40.680
storytelling plan concocted by show creators
David Benioff and D.B. Weiss.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.940
In 2016, Benioff told Deadline:

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.620
To that end, Game of Thrones certainly wasn't
like other TV shows, which often consist of

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.260
self-contained episodes or season-long plot
arcs.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.400
As Benioff explained:

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:03.360
Whether or not they succeeded is something
you'll ultimately have to decide for yourself.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.919
At some point, History Channel realized that
historically-based TV programming could include

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.980
more than just World War II documentaries.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.300
In 2013, the channel delved into scripted
shows for the first time with Vikings.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:19.140
Possibly the most violent TV series that's
also ostensibly educational, the drama explored

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.039
the lives, families, and seafaring fights
of eighth-century Norse warrior Ragnar Lothbrok

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.040
and his kin.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.360
It's just like Game of Thrones…except possibly
sort of real!

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:41.540
Creator and writer Michael Hirst based the
show on the written sagas of Lothbrok, so

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:45.940
Vikings is sorta-kinda based on a true story…and
a true story that ends.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.340
All Hirst set out to do was tell the complete
tale.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.880
And over the course of six well-regarded seasons,
he did just that.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.440
The decision to end Vikings was ultimately
his own.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.080
In January 2019, he told Variety:

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.420
Over the last decade, USA has established
itself as a major player in quality cable

00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:16.990
television, offering viewers plenty of breezy,
almost airless dramas that usually involve

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.160
some element of the law, and lots of extremely
attractive people.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.720
The network's slate of hits includes Psych,
White Collar, Royal Pains, and Suits…the

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.420
last of which centers around Mike Ross, portrayed
by Patrick J. Adams.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:32.440
He's a fiercely intelligent man and quite
the brilliant attorney…despite the fact

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.620
that he doesn't even have a law degree to
his name.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:39.280
Winning case after case while maintaining
his secret, Mike soon meets his match in paralegal

00:07:39.289 --> 00:07:41.629
Rachel Zane, portrayed by Meghan Markle.

00:07:41.629 --> 00:07:46.060
The show's juicy legal and romantic storylines
brought in millions of fans…and according

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.560
to The Sun, a certain Prince Harry was among
them.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.440
When Markle began dating Prince Harry in 2016,
it spelled the eventual end of the show.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.260
As you probably know, the couple fell in love
and got married in a royal wedding for the

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:58.720
ages in 2018.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.720
"All that I am I give to you."

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:05.180
Presumably because she'd be living in England
and devoting her life to charity work and

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.980
public appearances, Markle left Suits at the
end of the show's seventh season.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:13.099
Adams evidently figured that was a good time
to leave too, and Suits' writers crafted a

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:17.210
sweet exit for the couple: They got married
and moved to Seattle to run a law firm.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.860
"You're the husband I've always wanted.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.160
And I can't wait to begin our adventure together."

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:28.540
Suits returned for Season 8 in 2018, without
two of its main characters…and the departure

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.120
of Markle and Ross proved too much of a loss
to weather.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.520
Producers called it a day, choosing to end
the show in 2019 after the ninth and final

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:36.840
season of 10 episodes.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:41.820
Now, they're reportedly focusing on Pearson,
a Suits spinoff starring Gina Torres's character.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.080
HBO isn't the only place to find quality programming
on a cable network for a few extra bucks a

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.080
month.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.320
In recent years, Showtime has also made a
name for itself with critically acclaimed

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:55.560
and award-winning fare like Homeland, Shameless,
Ray Donovan, and The Affair.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:01.100
In 2017, Showtime debuted SMILF, a dark, gritty
comedy from the mind of actress Frankie Shaw.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:06.080
The Mr. Robot co-star created, wrote, directed,
and starred as Bridgette, the titular single

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.920
South Boston mother with a sex drive and a
social life.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.860
"I am sorry."

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.100
"I'm sorry."

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.680
"Okay, I'm sorry.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:14.880
No, I'm sorry."

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:16.380
"I'm sorry."

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:17.500
"No, I'm sorry."

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.760
The show attracted a sizable audience and
a second season was quickly ordered, but some

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.120
serious controversy eventually reared its
head…and it directly led to SMILF's early

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:27.420
and abrupt demise.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:32.400
In December 2018, SMILF writers reported to
the Writers Guild of America that Shaw had

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.620
allegedly separated staffers by race.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.780
Meanwhile, co-star Samara Weaving claimed
that Shaw forced her to film a nude scene

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:41.180
despite her "no nudity" contract clause.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.240
In March 2019, the network said in a statement
that:

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.880
And that, as they say, was that.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.400
Transparent aggressively evolved over the
course of three seasons.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:59.720
The quiet, ultra-subtle Amazon series took
on a premise that had never been fully explored

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:00.920
on American television.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:05.400
Jeffrey Tambor starred as Maura Pfefferman,
a college professor who finally starts living

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.440
her truth: She identifies as a woman.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.300
In other words, she's a "trans parent."

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.440
Over the course of four seasons, Transparent
ambitiously expanded into a show that was

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.440
not only about gender identity, but sexual
and cultural identity, too...particularly

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:21.620
in how the Pfefferman family explored their
Jewish faith and background.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.380
But Maura's journey remained the core theme
of the show…that is, until producers and

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:26.850
Amazon fired Tambor.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:31.770
The company launched an investigation after
cast member Trace Lysette accused Tambor of

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:34.920
multiple acts of sexual harassment, and one
instance of assault.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.120
Tabor's assistant Van Barnes also accused
the actor of inappropriate behavior.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:45.740
"It ran the gamut of sexual harassment, physical
harrassment, and verbal abuse constantly."

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:50.280
With Tambor gone, the show's writers decided
to wrap up the series with a special two-hour

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:54.920
finale that's set to air on Amazon in September
2019, according to Variety.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.880
The death of Maura will figure prominently
in the storyline... and the episode is actually

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.220
going to be a musical, if you can believe
it.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:05.880
"Let's take it from the top."

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.240
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.640
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
shows are coming soon.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:16.180
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

